.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt OPENDIRECTORYD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm opendirectoryd
.Nd local directory service daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
keeps the local users and groups and answers for them.
It serves three Mach services:
the Open Directory API that the CFOpenDirectory framework uses,
the user and group lookups of the C library, such as
.Xr getpwnam 3
and
.Xr getgrouplist 3 ,
and the membership calls of
.Xr mbr_check_membership 3 .
Lookups of other kinds, such as services and hosts, are not answered,
so the C library takes them from the files in
.Pa /etc .
.Pp
.Nm
runs as root and does not fork.
.Pa /etc/rc
starts it in the background.
It logs through
.Xr syslog 3 .
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Log to the standard error instead of
.Xr syslog 3 .
.El
.Ss Nodes
The node
.Pa /Local/Default
holds the records.
The search node
.Pa /Search
and the authentication search node show the same records.
A record found through
.Pa /Search
names
.Pa /Local/Default
as its AppleMetaNodeLocation.
Changes go to
.Pa /Local/Default
only.
.Ss Records
Each record is a binary property list:
.Pa /var/db/dslocal/nodes/Default/users/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .plist
for a user and
.Pa /var/db/dslocal/nodes/Default/groups/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .plist
for a group.
It is a dictionary from attribute names to arrays of strings or data.
The names are the short ones of the local store, which map to the
standard attributes as follows:
.Bl -column "authentication_authority" "AuthenticationAuthority" -offset indent
.It Sy Key Ta Sy Attribute
.It name Ta RecordName
.It uid Ta UniqueID
.It gid Ta PrimaryGroupID
.It realname Ta RealName
.It home Ta NFSHomeDirectory
.It home_loc Ta HomeDirectory
.It shell Ta UserShell
.It passwd Ta Password
.It generateduid Ta GeneratedUID
.It authentication_authority Ta AuthenticationAuthority
.It users Ta GroupMembership
.It groupmembers Ta GroupMembers
.It nestedgroups Ta NestedGroups
.El
.Pp
Other standard attributes, such as Comment, EMailAddress and Picture,
have short keys of their own.
Any other key is the native attribute
.Li dsAttrTypeNative: Ns Ar key .
RecordType, AppleMetaNodeLocation and AppleMetaRecordName are not stored.
Every record a query returns carries RecordType, RecordName and
AppleMetaNodeLocation.
.Pp
A record must have a name.
Files that are not regular files, files over 1 MiB and files that do
not parse are left out.
Files whose names start with a dot are temporary, and are removed when
.Nm
starts.
.Nm
reads the files again when a file is added to, removed from or renamed in
the users or groups directory.
It also reads them again after the clock is stepped.
A file changed in place may not be read again until one of these
happens, or until
.Nm
restarts.
It writes a record to a new file, syncs it and renames it over the old
one, so a crash leaves the old or the new record.
Record files are mode 0600 and the directories 0700.
After each change
.Nm
posts the directory service cache notifications for users and groups.
.Ss Passwords
A password is kept in the native attribute ShadowHashData.
Its value is a binary property list with one key,
SALTED-SHA512-PBKDF2, whose dictionary holds
.Li entropy ,
128 bytes of PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA512 output,
.Li salt ,
32 random bytes, and
.Li iterations ,
40000 for new passwords.
AuthenticationAuthority is then
.Li ;ShadowHash;HASHLIST:<SALTED-SHA512-PBKDF2>
and passwd is
.Li ******** .
A record without ShadowHashData has no password.
An AuthenticationAuthority that starts with
.Li ;DisabledUser;
disables the account.
.Pp
Password checks take as long for a missing user or a missing hash as
for a wrong password.
After five failed checks for one user, each further check must wait 2,
4, 8, 16 and 32 seconds, then 60 seconds.
A check made too soon fails with the account temporarily locked and the
number of seconds left.
A good password resets the count.
The count is kept in memory, and is forgotten 15 minutes after the last
failure.
Failures for names that are not users are counted apart from those of
users, so they cannot push a user's count out.
A count is never dropped while its wait runs.
A user's count that has reached five is kept until it is forgotten.
.Ss Authorization
.Nm
takes the caller's identity from the audit token of each request.
.Bl -bullet
.It
Anyone may read records.
ShadowHashData is returned only to root, and only when asked for by name.
.It
Anyone may check a password and ask whether an account may log in.
.It
Root may change anything.
A node with the credentials of root or of a member of the admin group may
also change anything.
.It
A user may change their own password with the old one.
Only root, or a node with the credentials of root or of a member of
the admin group, may set a password without the old one.
.It
A user may change their own RealName, Building, PhoneNumber,
HomePhoneNumber and Picture.
.It
A user may change their own UserShell to a shell listed in
.Pa /etc/shells ,
if their current shell is listed there too.
Only root and the admin group may change a restricted shell, or a
missing one.
.El
.Pp
A node takes a user's credentials only with the right password, and
not when the account is disabled or has expired.
The credentials lapse when the account is disabled or expires, or when
its password changes.
The node then acts for its caller alone.
.Ss Lookups
Users and groups listed in
.Pa /etc/passwd ,
.Pa /etc/master.passwd
or
.Pa /etc/group
are left out of the user and group lists, since the C library lists those
files too.
They are still found by name and number.
A group list starts with the user's primary group.
Group membership comes from GroupMembership, GroupMembers, the primary
group and NestedGroups.
Every user is a member of the group with number 12.
.Ss The kernel's identity resolver
The kernel checks access control lists by GUID.
.Nm
registers with the kernel as its identity resolver.
Then the entries that
.Xr chmod 1
adds for users and groups match.
A thread answers the kernel's requests.
It turns a user or group number, GUID, NT SID or name into the others,
and says whether a user is a member of a group.
Membership is found as for the membership calls, nested groups included.
A user number with no record is a member of no group.
A user or group without a GeneratedUID has a GUID made from its number.
The NT SID is the SMBSID attribute, where a record has one.
.Pp
.Nm
clears the kernel's cache when it registers, after each change it makes,
and again a second later.
The kernel keeps most answers for up to 300 seconds, and some until its
cache is cleared.
After editing the record files by hand, restart
.Nm .
The thread reads the records on its own, so a slow request to the other
services does not hold up the kernel.
.Pp
Only root may register.
If registration fails,
.Nm
logs it and serves the other services.
If the kernel drops it, it registers again, up to five times in a row.
On
.Dv SIGTERM
or
.Dv SIGINT
it deregisters and exits.
.Ss Limits
A request to the Open Directory API may be at most 1 MiB.
A request that is larger, that does not parse, or that would take more
than 64 KiB plus 32 times its size in memory fails with a parameter error.
A change that would make a record file larger than 1 MiB fails with a
schema error, and the record stays as it was.
UserShell, RealName, Building, PhoneNumber, HomePhoneNumber and Picture
take at most 16 values of at most 1024 bytes each.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa /var/db/dslocal/nodes/Default/users
user records
.It Pa /var/db/dslocal/nodes/Default/groups
group records
.It Pa /etc/shells
the shells a user may choose
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dscl 1 ,
.Xr getpwnam 3 ,
.Xr mbr_check_membership 3
